<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-14">
  <title>Week 14: The Generalized Riemann Integral (8.1)</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-generalized-Riemann-integral">
    <title>The Generalized Riemann Integral</title>

    <p>
        We saw in the last section that not every derivative has an integral. Ideally we want to be able to integrate every derivative so that we can state the FTC with no assumptions on integrability.
    </p>

    <p>
        The Lebesgue integral was introduced in 1904 to integrate functions that can't be integrated using Riemann integration, but it isn't perfect. There are functions that can't be integrated using the Lebesgue integral.
    </p>

    <p>
        In particular one cannot have the FTC with the Lebesgue integral without some additional restriction of the functions.
    </p>

    <p>
        In 1960 Jaroslav Kurzweil and Ralph Henstock independently developed a new defintion of the integral called the generalized Riemann integral or the Kurzweil-Henstock integral.
    </p>

    <p>
        The generalized Riemann integral can integrate even more functions than the Lebesgue integral. In addition using the generalized Riemann integral the FTC can be proven without any additional restrictions on the functions.
    </p>

    <p>
        Remarkably the generalized Riemann integral is defined by making a slight modification to Riemann's definition of the integral.
    </p>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-Riemann-integral">
        <title>The Riemann Integral</title>

        <definition xml:id="def-tagged-partition">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let $P= \{x_0,x_1,x_2, \ldots, x_n\}$ be a partition of $[a,b]$. A <term>tagged partition</term> is one where in addition to $P$ we have chosen a point $c_k$ from each subinterval $[x_{k-1}, x_k]$
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            Example: Let $P = \{0,3,5,9,10\}$ be a partition of $[0,10]$. An example of a tagged partition is $(P, \{c_k\}_{k=1}^4)$ where $c_1 = 3$, $c_2 = 5$, $c_3 = 9$, and $c_4= 10$.
        </p>

        <p>
            Another example of a tagged partition is $(P, \{c_k\}_{k=1}^4)$ where $c_1 = 2$, $c_2 = 5$, $c_3 = 5$, and $c_4= 9.9$.
        </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-Riemann-sum">
        <statement>
            <p>
               Given a function $f$ and a tagged partition $(P, \{c_k\}_{k=1}^n)$ the <term>Riemann sum</term> generated by this partition is given by 
               <md>
                 R(f,P) = \sum_{k=1}^n f(c_k) (x_k-x_{k-1}).
               </md>
               
            </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        The idea of the Riemann sum is that we approximate the area under the curve by rectangles with width $(x_k-x_{k-1})$ and height $f(c_k)$.
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-delta-fine">
        <statement>
            <p>
               A partition is said to be $\delta$-fine if every subinterval$[x_{k-1},x_k]$ satisfies $x_k-x_{k-1} &lt; \delta$. In other words, every subinterval has width less than $\delta$. 
            </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <definition xml:id="def-riemann-integrable-2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A function is <term>Riemann integrable</term> if every Riemann sum converges to the same value as the widths of the subintervals tend to $0$.
            </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <definition xml:id="def-limit-criterion-riemann">
        <title>Limit Criterion for Riemann Integrability</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                A bounded function $f:[a,b] \to \mathbf{R}$ is Riemann integrable with integral 
                <md>
                     \int_a^b f(x)dx = A
                </md>
                if for every $\epsilon &gt; 0$ there exists $\delta &gt;0$ such that, for every $\delta$-fine tagged partition $(P, \{c_k\}_{k=1}^n)$ it follows that
                <md>
                    |R(f,P)-A| &lt; \epsilon.
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>
        
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-gauges">
        <title>Gauges and <m>\delta</m>-fine Partitions</title>

        <p>
            In the Riemann integral $\delta$ only depends on $\epsilon$ and is constant throughout the partition.
        </p>

        <p>
            We will modify the Riemann integral by letting $\delta(x)$ be a function of $x$. Thus $\delta(x)$ depends on both $x$ and $\epsilon$.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-gauge">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A function $\delta:[a,b] \to \mathbf{R}$ is called a <term>gauge</term> on $[a,b]$ if $\delta(x) &gt; 0$ on $[a,b]$.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-delta-fine-2">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Given a particular gauge $\delta(x)$ a tagged partition $(P, \{c_k\}_{k=1}^n)$ is $\delta(x)$-fine if every subinterval $[x_k, x_{k-1}]$ satisfies $x_k - x_{k-1} &lt; \delta(c_k)$. In other words, each subinterval $[x_k, x_{k-1}]$ has width less than $\delta(c_k)$.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            If $\delta(x)$ is a constant function, then this is the same definition as a $\delta$-fine partition.
        </p>

        <p>
            Example: Consider the interval $[0,10]$ and let $\delta(x) = \frac{x+2}{2}$. Let $P = \{0, 1 ,3,6,10\}$ with tags $c_1 = 1$, $c_2 = 3$, $c_3 = 5$, and $c_4 = 8$. Is $(P, \{c_k\}_{k=1}^4)$ a $\delta(x)$-fine partition?
        </p>

        <p>
            Example: Consider the interval $[0,10]$ and let $\delta(x) = \frac{x+2}{2}$. Let $P = \{0, 1 ,3,6,10\}$ with tags $c_1 = 1$, $c_2 = 2$, $c_3 = 6$, and $c_4 = 10$. Is $(P, \{c_k\}_{k=1}^4)$ a $\delta(x)$-fine partition?
        </p>

        <p>
            Given a gauge $\delta(x)$, it is not immediately clear that it is possible to find a $\delta(x)$ fine partition. The next theorem shows that in fact this is always possible.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-delta-fine">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Given a gauge $\delta(x)$ on an interval $[a,b]$, there exists a tagged partition $(P, \{c_k\}_{k=1}^n)$ that is $\delta(x)$-fine.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <proof>
            <p>
                We will do a proof by contradiction. Assume that $I_0 = [a,b]$ does not have a $\delta(x)$-fine partition.
            </p>

            <p>
                Let $c = \frac{1}{2}(a+b)$. If $[a,c]$ and $[c,b]$ both have $\delta(x)$-fine partitions $(P_1, \{c_k\})$ and $(P_2, \{d_k\})$ then the partition $P= P_1 \cup P_2$ is a $\delta(x)$-fine partition of $[a,b]$. 
            </p>

            <p>
                Thus at least one of $[a,c]$ and $[c,b]$ must not have a $\delta(x)$-fine partition.
            </p>

            <p>
                Let $I_1$ denote a subinterval that does not have a $\delta(x)$-fine partition.
            </p>

            <p>
                If we subdivide $I_1$ into two equal parts similar reasoning shows that at least one part much not have a $\delta(x)$-fine partition. Let $I_2$ denote this subinterval.
            </p>

            <p>
                Continuing in this way we can construct a nested sequence $\{I_n\}$ of subintervals so that the length of $I_n$ is $(b-a)/2^n$ and so that no $I_n$ has a $\delta(x)$-fine partition. Suppose $I_n = [a_n, b_n]$.
            </p>

            <p>
                By the nested interval property there exists $c \in \bigcap_{n=0}^{\infty} I_n$. Hence $c \in I_n$ for all $n$.
            </p>

            <p>
                If $\frac{b-a}{2^n}&lt; \delta(c)$ then $I_n$ would have a $\delta(x)$-fine partition given by $P = \{a_n, b_n\}$ with tag $c$. Hence $\delta(c) \leq \frac{b-a}{2^n}$ for all $n$.
            </p>

            <p>
                Letting $n \to \infty$ implies that $\delta(c) = 0$, which contradicts the fact that $\delta(x)$ is a gauge. Hence $I_0$ has a $\delta(x)$-fine partition.
            </p>
        </proof>
        
        <p>
            In the previous proof we showed the existence of a $\delta(x)$-fine partition by showing that it is impossible for it to not exist, but we didn't actually find a $\delta(x)$-fine partition.  
        </p>

        <p>
            The mathematical philosophy of constructivism asserts that one must actually find the desired object in order to prove that it exists, a proof by contradiction is not good enough. However, only a minority of mathematicians hold this view.
        </p>

    
          
                <p>
                   <em> Taking the principle of excluded middle from the mathematician would be the same, say, as proscribing the telescope to the astronomer or to the boxer the use of his fists</em>  -- David Hilbert
                </p>
               
           
    
        
    </subsection>

    </section>